    "durationMs": 118233,
    "finalText": "The URL shortener is complete and fully verified. Implementation:\n\n- **`server.js`** — Express 5.3.0 app using `pg` (node-postgres) as the persistence layer, reading `DATABASE_URL` from the environment. Routes: `POST /links` (201 with generated or custom code, 409 on duplicate custom code, 422 for malformed JSON / missing or non-http(s) / hostless URLs / invalid codes), `GET /links` (ascending creation order with `items` + `total`), `GET /links/{code}` (200/404), `GET /r/{code}` (atomic `UPDATE ... RETURNING` 302 redirect that increments clicks exactly once). Generated codes are 8 chars from a 64-char alphabet (uniform `byte % 64`), with retry on the unique-constraint violation.\n- **`migrations/0001_create_links.sql`** + **`migrate.js`** — a runner that tracks applied migrations in a `schema_migrations` table, so `start.sh` only applies pending ones.\n- **`start.sh`** — executable; runs migrations then `exec`s the server in the foreground on 0.0.0.0:8000 (dependencies were installed now, not at start).\n- **`README.md`** — start instructions and API summary.\n\nI tested every requirement end to end: all status codes (201/302/404/409/422), click increments, ascending list order, and a full restart cycle confirming both links and click counts persist in Postgres with migrations skipped on re-run."
  }
